   517	    def _reconcile(self) -> None:
   518	        """Refuse to stamp past a durable line the index has not consumed.
   519	
   520	        A failure *after* the fsync (sqlite error, power cut, the process
   521	        dying) rolls the transaction back, leaving the line durable and the
   522	        offset unstamped — that is the recoverable state, and the next open
   523	        tails it forward. But a long-lived handle never reopens: it would
   524	        append line N+1 and stamp the offset past *both*, burying the orphan
   525	        forever while reporting ``synced``. So every append reconciles first.
   526	
   527	        Cheap in the common case: one ``store_meta`` read and one ``stat``,
   528	        no scan, no lock. Only a disagreement pays for :meth:`catch_up`
   529	        (which also covers a shrunk log, a torn tail and untrustworthy
   530	        offset metadata). Two handles racing the same orphan line is a loud
   531	        failure, not a silent one: the loser's tail INSERT hits the primary
   532	        key, rolls back, and the line stays recoverable.
